other hot hatches out there but  she would like to see a bit more  attention to detail in the interior all  in all though she says the kia has got  it right with a new cgt  another thing that appeals to her is the  price  the top spec gt track version that we  tested is available in germany for  around 27 000 euros  mercedes has lifted the covers on the  replacement for the viano people carrier  the new v-class shares many styling cues  with its predecessor but the biggest  changes can be found on the inside  it's leather seats and sweeping dash  wouldn't look out of place in a mercedes  c-class the new model will hit showrooms  in may and is expected to retail for  around 41 000 euros in germany  volkswagen is raising the stakes in the  hot hatch segment with its revised golf  r with 221 kilowatts and 380 newton  meters of torque going through a  four-wheel drive system the new r  promises to be a blast come winter  its dsg 6-speed dual clutch automatic  transmission helps it to get from zero  to 100 kilometers an hour in just 4.9  seconds  it'd be yours for around 38 000 euros in  germany  old vw buses like the t2 have legions of  fans the last one rolled off the  production lines in germany in 1979  but in sao paulo brazil production of  the t2 continued right up until the end  of last year  it was a huge success during its  lifetime for a whopping 56 years an  average of 20 000 rolled off the  assembly line every year  now it's the end of an era the van got  all dressed up for its swan song with a  limited edition model  volkswagen sales director jochen funk  says the old gal is a very specific  product some brazilians regard the vw as  a brazilian brand of german descent  they  at first vw's had to be imported to  brazil but in 1957 a plant in oshieta  began building the t2's predecessor the  t1  demand grew rapidly from just 370 sold  in the first year to over 41 000 four  years later production of the t2 began  in 1975  signaling the start of a success story  that lasted for almost four decades  without changing the van's basic  character vw constantly optimized the  vehicle  the limited last edition model was  originally envisioned as a batch of just  600 but demand was so great that vw  doubled that to 1200.  maurizio augusto marx is the proud owner  of one of the first vw vans ever built  and spent a year and a half restoring  the vehicle with original parts  he replaced the headlights and the  engine he says there are only four 1950  panel vans in brazil  and only 36 of them in the whole world  in brazil the van is a volkswagen in the  literal sense it's a real people's car  the multi-purpose vehicle can still be  seen on almost every street corner there  are no limits to the inventive spirit  with which people modify it the t2 is  often seen with the most wacky  modifications it can be a police car a  fire truck a mobile snack bar a tv  broadcasting studio or an ambulance  the vw van is good for just about  everything  everyone loved this jack of all trades  but it wasn't able to keep up with the  times stricter safety and emission  regulations helped kill off the cult  classic  jochen funk explains that the van was  designed more than 60 years ago when  airbags and advanced braking systems  didn't exist  even the best engineers can't fit these  upgrades into a vw bus  so now regulations mean that the company  had to end production despite continuing  high demand  the final last edition model number 1200  was never put up for sale it was  carefully packed up and sent by  freighter home to wolfsburg germany  a special place was waiting for it at  the volkswagen auto museum here the t2  enjoys its retirement among its  relatives  head of the museum's vintage car team is  sad that this perennial icon is no  longer being manufactured he's glad that  the last of the species is in his own  hands but says that is meager  consolation  is  the t2 is going nowhere soon with such a  loyal following plenty of existing ones  won't be jumped or retired for a long  time to come  chevrolet's corvette stingray still has  a dedicated cult following in the early  1960s chevy one of america's big four  set out to give established sports car  makers like jaguar and maserati a run  for their money  the new corvette's design reflected the  typical sports car styling of the time a  long engine compartment with a snappy  fastback  the shape character and image of this  automotive legend were american through  and through until the arrival of the  dodge viper in 1992 the corvette was the  only true sports car made entirely in  the usa  under the hood of course the stingray  had a v8 packing 250 horsepower in the  basic version  an optional extra was a four speed stick  shift which was a usual option for an  american car  the stingray was also the first corvette  to offer two different body styles a  coupet and a convertible  chevrolet fitted successive models of  the stingray with bigger and bigger  engines  the v8 big block version sported a  business-like but fake air scoop on the  hood  throughout its five-year run the  stingray's basic form remained almost  unchanged that was also unusual in 1960s  america where new models appeared every  year  time now for a trip back in time the  corvette appeared a decade before the  stingray hit showrooms chevrolet  unveiled the first model  now known as the c1 to an admiring  public at the general motors motorama in  1953.  chevrolet engineer and race car driver  zora arcos duntov is known as the father  of the corvette he mounted a lean  two-seater fiberglass body on a robust  box frame a concept that has survived to  the current generation of corvettes  the corvette didn't get its famous v8  edge until 1956.  after that marriage the street cruiser  was transformed into a serious sports  car  in 1960 a corvette team entered the le  mans 24-hour race against the european  competition  taking eighth place overall but first in  its class  it was a good showing for the new kid on  the block  by the early 60s the original corvette  was beginning to look past its sell by  date the design department kicked around  various ideas until head designer bill  mitchell got inspiration for the first  corvette coupe  from an actual stingray the new stingray  was even sportier leaner and meaner  47 year old engineer oliver tripp is a  corvette fan body and soul he collects  everything connected with this  automotive icon  his impressive collection includes  corvettes from 1964 to 1996.  but his favorite is the c2 the stingray  model built from 1963-67  whenever he finds some time in his busy  schedule he takes one of his treasures  out for a spin  he says he dreamt of corvettes even as a  child he saw one as a small boy and  immediately fell in love with it  eventually he began collecting them  whenever he could he admits it's a  typical male dream  he explains that an american car  collector circles the c2 is the usa's  answer to the portion it handles very  well in comparison to its contemporaries  and relative to other vintage classic  sports cars it's still affordable  oliver trev had his blue 65 coupe  completely restored and customized to  his own specifications in the united  states  but corvette fan though he is he says  the performance of the big block v8 is  only just enough  american car fans call a corvette like  oliver's a sleeper that means it has  lots of power under the hood but  everything else is like the original  nowadays a stingray in good condition  can run a good 50 000 euros the most  sought after models are the big blocks  and split window coupes the best place  to find one if at all is in the united  states  you


